it, SPECA). **Pipeline:** 1. Extract `(finding_description, vulnerable_code_snippet, severity, vulnerability_type)` from structured reports. Bastet's two-layer taxonomy (46 Tags × 77 Subtags) gives fine-grained type labels. 2. For each finding, locate the **fix commit** in the audited repository (most Code4rena repos link fixes in the report or contest resolution). 3. Extract the `(vulnerable_contract, patched_contract)` pair. 4. **Verify:** compile both versions with the target compiler; run the protocol's existing test suite on both; confirm the vulnerability-specific test (if provided) pass …(+3377 chars)


ulnerable_code) → (vulnerability_type, explanation, patched_code)`. This mirrors the [Decoupled Audit Framework](https://arxiv.org/abs/2606.03128)'s approach: teacher (Qwen3-30B) → student (Qwen3-0.6B) with Chain-of-Verification. This is explicitly a **code-to-fix** distillation: the model learns to produce both a diagnosis and a verified patch. **Estimated yield:** 3,000–5,000 verified distillation pairs. ### 5.6 Data Ingredient 5: Benign Contract Corpus (Negative Samples) **Source:** SmartBugs-Wild (47,398 contracts), supplemented with Etherscan-verified contracts deployed after Jan 2024 (to …(+3391 chars)


